iding accurate counseling.\n\n**Discontinue lisinopril if cough develops.**\n• Trap: There is a common belief that all patients on ACE inhibitors will experience cough.\n• Why it fails here: This patient has not reported a cough, and discontinuing lisinopril without symptoms could lead to uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Remember: Recognizing that not all patients will experience side effects is crucial in medication management.\n\n**Avoid grapefruit juice while taking amlodipine.**\n• Trap: Students might think that all calcium channel blockers interact with grapefruit juice.\n• Why it fails here: While grapefruit juice can affect some medications, amlodipine is not significantly impacted by it, making this advice misleading.\n• Remember: Understanding specific drug interactions is vital for accurate patient counseling.\n## Key takeaway\n**When counseling patients on amlodipine, prioritize monitoring for peripheral edema, especially in those with a history of this side effect.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Remember: Amlodipine can cause **swelling**; keep an eye on those **feet**!","kind":"delete_generic+dup"},{"id":"cmqvnn4yg00151yjaf1dgalei","old_md5":"c4ef318698c2c9ecb6ff1df9e3045492","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nAdding glipizide to current therapy is the best choice for better glycemic control.\n• **Combination therapy** with metformin and glipizide is effective for type 2 diabetes management.\n• The patient's **HbA1c of 8.5%** indicates that additional therapy is necessary.\n• Glipizide can help lower blood glucose levels and improve overall **glycemic control**.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Continue with metformin only.**\n• Trap: Students may think that maintaining the current regimen is sufficient.\n• Why it fails here: However, the patient's HbA1c of 8.5% shows that metformin alone is not adequately controlling his diabetes, necessitating additional therapy.\n• Remember: This highlights the importance of adjusting treatment based on glycemic control metrics.\n\n**Add glimepiride instead of glipizide.**\n• Trap: There may be confusion about the need to switch medications rather than adding to the current regimen.\n• Why it fails here: Glimepiride is an alternative sulfonylurea, but glipizide is already prescribed, and switching is unnecessary when the patient can benefit from the current therapy.\n• Remember: This emphasizes the need to evaluate the patient's existing treatment plan before making changes.\n\n**Switch to insulin therapy.**\n• Trap: Students might think that insulin is the only option for better control in patients with high HbA1c.\n• Why it fails here: While insulin can be effective, it is not the first-line option for this patient who can still benefit from oral medications like glipizide.\n• Remember: This illustrates the importance of considering less invasive options before escalating to insulin therapy.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing type 2 diabetes, always assess the patient's HbA1c and consider adding medications rather than switching or stopping effective treatments.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'Add, don't switch' when a patient's diabetes isn't controlled.","kind":"delete_generic+dup"},{"id":"cmqvnn5ta001e1yja9ly6ejon","old_md5":"8977aa8c706bb816c8ca500d7fdace3e","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nTaking metformin with food is essential to reduce gastrointestinal side effects.\n• **Metformin** can cause nausea and diarrhea, which are common side effects.\n• Taking it with **food** helps to minimize these gastrointestinal issues.\n• Proper **counseling** ensures better adherence to the medication regimen.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means patients are more likely to continue their medication without interruption, leading to better management of their diabetes.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means patients are more likely to continue their medication without interruption, leading to better management of their diabetes.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\