 less relevant at this moment.\n• Remember: Focusing on dependence shifts attention away from the critical need to discuss the delayed onset of antidepressant effects.\n\n**Anxiety symptoms will resolve immediately after starting medication.**\n• Trap: Students may mistakenly believe that patients expect quick relief from anxiety symptoms.\n• Why it fails here: This statement is misleading; patients should be informed that it typically takes weeks for medications to exert their full effects.\n• Remember: This option contradicts the essential understanding that managing anxiety requires patience and realistic expectations about treatment timelines.\n## Key takeaway\n**When counseling patients about antidepressants, emphasize that effects take time to develop.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'patience is key' when starting antidepressants for anxiety.","kind":"delete_generic+dup"},{"id":"cmr0b7pua005e1yq31hjzrn3b","old_md5":"9032b17035d94b7be396e957576cddc1","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nAssessing the need for a maintenance therapy adjustment is the best next step for this patient.\n• **Inhaler overuse** indicates poor control of COPD.\n• A **thorough assessment** is essential before making medication changes.\n• Adjustments may include **increasing doses** or adding medications based on evaluation.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means we prioritize understanding the patient's current management before making changes, ensuring that we address the root cause of their symptoms effectively.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means we prioritize understanding the patient's current management before making changes, ensuring that we address the root cause of their symptoms effectively.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Increase the dose of fluticasone/salmeterol.**\n• Trap: Students may think that simply increasing the dose will solve the problem.\n• Why it fails here: Without assessing the patient's overall symptom control and inhaler technique, inc