mine**\n• Trap: Students may think this is a good choice because it is commonly used as a sleep aid.\n• Why it fails here: However, diphenhydramine can increase sedation and may interact with fluoxetine, raising the risk of side effects like confusion or dizziness.\n• Remember: This highlights the importance of considering drug interactions when recommending OTC medications.\n\n**Doxylamine**\n• Trap: This option might be chosen due to its sedative properties, similar to diphenhydramine.\n• Why it fails here: Doxylamine also poses a risk of increased sedation and potential interactions with fluoxetine, which can be problematic for this patient.\n• Remember: Understanding the sedative effects and interactions of OTC medications is crucial in patient care.\n\n**Valerian root**\n• Trap: Students may consider valerian root a natural alternative without recognizing its efficacy and safety profile.\n• Why it fails here: Valerian root lacks robust evidence for effectiveness and can also interact with fluoxetine, making it a less reliable choice.\n• Remember: Evaluating the evidence and safety of herbal supplements is essential when recommending treatments.\n## Key takeaway\n**When recommending OTC sleep aids, prioritize options with minimal drug interactions and proven efficacy.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'M' for Melatonin: Minimal interactions and Maximum safety!","kind":"lead_in+dup"},{"id":"cmr0tgx7m001h1yoit73hag2b","old_md5":"6c69944efc902f3197534a6c5dc984a7","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nTaking metformin with food can significantly reduce gastrointestinal side effects.\n• **Gastrointestinal side effects** like nausea and diarrhea are common with metformin.\n• Taking metformin **with food** can help minimize these effects.\n• Patients should be counseled on **proper administration** to enhance adherence.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Discontinue metformin if diarrhea persists for more than 24 hours.**\n• Trap: Students may think that stopping 