arly beneficial for patients with **type 2 diabetes** who are concerned about weight.\n• These medications can enhance **satiety** (feeling of fullness), leading to reduced caloric intake.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means that when counseling patients, we can reassure them that GLP-1 receptor agonists not only help manage blood sugar but also support weight management, which is crucial for overall health.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means that when counseling patients, we can reassure them that GLP-1 receptor agonists not only help manage blood sugar but also support weight management, which is crucial for overall health.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**GLP-1 receptor agonists are not effective for weight loss.**\n• Trap: Students might think that GLP-1 receptor agonists do not aid in weight management.\n• Why it fails here: This option fails because GLP-1 receptor agonists are clinically proven to assist with weight loss in patients with type 2 diabetes.\n• Remember: Understanding the dual benefits of GLP-1 receptor agonists is essential for effective diabetes management.\n\n**Weight gain is a common side effect of GLP-1 receptor agonists.**\n• Trap: There is a belief that all diabetes medications lead to weight gain, including GLP-1 receptor agonists.\n• Why it fails here: This option is incorrect as GLP-1 receptor agonists are associated with weight loss rather than weight gain.\n• Remember: Recognizing the unique profile of GLP-1 receptor agonists helps in addressing patient concerns about weight.\n\n**Metformin should be discontinued when starting a GLP-1 receptor agonist.**\n• Trap: Some may think that starting a new medication requires stopping existing ones.\n• Why it fails here: This option is wrong because metformin is often continued alongside GLP-1 receptor agonists to enhance glycemic control.\n• Remember: Understanding the complementary roles of diabetes medications is key to optimizing treatment.\n## Key takeaway\n**Whe