ctice, this means you can confidently prescribe liraglutide knowing it won't exacerbate the patient's CKD or increase hypoglycemia risk, allowing for better diabetes management.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Glyburide**\n• Trap: Students pick this because they believe all sulfonylureas are effective for diabetes management.\n• Why it fails here: Glyburide is not recommended in this patient due to its high risk of **hypoglycemia**, particularly in those with renal impairment like CKD.\n• Remember: This highlights the importance of choosing medications that are safe for patients with compromised renal function.\n\n**Insulin glargine**\n• Trap: Students may think insulin is always a safe option for diabetes control.\n• Why it fails here: While insulin glargine can be effective, it carries a significant risk of hypoglycemia, especially in patients with CKD, making it less suitable for this patient.\n• Remember: This emphasizes the need to assess renal function when considering insulin therapy.\n\n**Dapagliflozin**\n• Trap: Students might assume that all SGLT2 inhibitors are safe regardless of kidney function.\n• Why it fails here: Dapagliflozin is contraindicated in patients with an eGFR below 60 mL/min, which applies to this patient with an eGFR of 55 mL/min.\n• Remember: This reinforces the importance of understanding renal dosing and contraindications for SGLT2 inhibitors.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing diabetes in patients with CKD, prioritize medications like liraglutide that minimize hypoglycemia risk.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'Lira for CKD' to remember that liraglutide is safe and effective for patients with chronic kidney disease.","kind":"lead_in+dup"},{"id":"cmr4cwe5q004o1yl8ua7i75kz","old_md5":"e672dc48d41d43d1f878a74bfcaf5fd3","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nMonitoring for signs of bleeding is critical for patients on warfarin.\n• **Warfarin** increases the risk of bleeding, necessitating careful monitoring.\n• Herbal supplements 