nsiderations.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'Bleeding first, then dosing' when counseling warfarin patients.","kind":"lead_in+dup"},{"id":"cmr4db6qy00031ywgrp7yc6x0","old_md5":"26a17bcfe239c5adea24de0785ee408a","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nThe combination of sitagliptin and glipizide poses the greatest risk of hypoglycemia.\n• **Sitagliptin** and **glipizide** both lower blood glucose levels.\n• Using them together increases the risk of **hypoglycemia** (low blood sugar).\n• Careful **monitoring** of blood glucose is essential when combining these medications.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means that patients on both medications need to be educated about recognizing hypoglycemia symptoms and should have their blood glucose levels checked regularly to prevent complications.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means that patients on both medications need to be educated about recognizing hypoglycemia symptoms and should have their blood glucose levels checked regularly to prevent complications.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Sitagliptin and metformin.**\n• Trap: Students may think this combination is risky due to both being diabetes medications.\n• Why it fails here: However, sitagliptin and metformin are often used together safely, as they have complementary mechanisms of action without significant interaction.\n• Remember: Understanding the mechanisms of diabetes medications helps clarify their safe combinations.\n\n**Glipizide and warfarin.**\n• Trap: There is a belief that all diabetes medications interact significantly with anticoagulants.\n• Why it fails here: While glipizide can affect blood sugar levels, it does not have a direct interaction with warfarin that would pose a significant risk in this scenario.\n• Remember: Recognizing that not all diabetes medications interact with anticoagulants is crucial for safe prescribing.\n\n**Metformin and lisinopril.**\n• Trap: Students might think that combining these two medications poses a