n lead to **increased symptoms** and reliance on rescue inhalers.\n• Ensuring the patient uses inhalers **correctly** is essential before adjusting medications.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means that ensuring proper inhaler technique can significantly improve a patient's symptom control and reduce the need for rescue medications.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means that ensuring proper inhaler technique can significantly improve a patient's symptom control and reduce the need for rescue medications.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Increase the dose of tiotropium**\n• Trap: Students may think that increasing the dose will directly improve symptom control.\n• Why it fails here: If the patient is not using the inhaler correctly, increasing the dose may not provide any benefit and could lead to unnecessary side effects.\n• Remember: This highlights the importance of confirming proper inhaler technique before making medication adjustments.\n\n**Switch to a different long-acting beta-agonist**\n• Trap: There may be an assumption that a different medication will resolve the patient's issues.\n• Why it fails here: Switching medications does not address the underlying problem of improper inhaler use, which is critical for effective treatment.\n• Remember: This reinforces the need to assess inhaler technique before considering medication changes.\n\n**Add a leukotriene receptor antagonist**\n• Trap: Students might believe that adding another medication will help manage the patient's symptoms.\n• Why it fails here: Adding a new medication without ensuring proper use of existing inhalers may not improve the patient's condition and could complicate her regimen.\n• Remember: This emphasizes the importance of optimizing current therapy before introducing additional medications.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing COPD, always assess inhaler technique before making any medication changes.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'Technique first, then Treatment' for e