cts.\n• Addressing side effects early can improve **adherence** to treatment.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means that patients may be more likely to continue their antidepressant therapy if side effects are managed effectively, leading to better overall outcomes.\n## Clinical pearl\nIn practice, this means that patients may be more likely to continue their antidepressant therapy if side effects are managed effectively, leading to better overall outcomes.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Continue omeprazole as needed for GERD symptoms.**\n• Trap: Students might think this option is sufficient for managing GI upset.\n• Why it fails here: While continuing omeprazole is appropriate for GERD, it does not specifically address the GI upset caused by sertraline, which is the patient's primary concern.\n• Remember: This highlights the importance of directly addressing medication side effects to improve patient comfort.\n\n**Monitor for signs of serotonin syndrome.**\n• Trap: Students may believe that monitoring for serotonin syndrome is the most critical action.\n• Why it fails here: Although monitoring for serotonin syndrome is important, it is not the immediate concern for this patient who is experiencing gastrointestinal upset from sertraline.\n• Remember: This emphasizes the need to prioritize management of side effects that directly affect the patient's quality of life.\n\n**Discontinue sertraline if gastrointestinal upset persists.**\n• Trap: Students might think that discontinuing the medication is the best solution for any side effects.\n• Why it fails here: Discontinuing sertraline without trying to manage the side effects first could lead to a relapse of anxiety and depression symptoms.\n• Remember: This option overlooks the importance of finding solutions to side effects before making drastic changes to treatment.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing side effects of medications like sertraline, prioritize strategies that enhance patient comfort and