uration to evaluate respiratory status over other vital signs.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing pneumonia, prioritize monitoring oxygen saturation to ensure adequate oxygen delivery and detect respiratory distress early.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Remember: In pneumonia, think 'O2 first' for oxygen saturation!","kind":"lead_in"},{"id":"cmr4itekm004o1y7ucki6vns2","old_md5":"17d03bf1c2d96d1c2cf6b2170eec2359","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nMonitoring for signs of bleeding is crucial for patients on apixaban.\n• **Apixaban** is an anticoagulant that increases the risk of bleeding.\n• Patients with **chronic kidney disease (CKD)** are at higher risk for bleeding complications.\n• Recognizing signs of bleeding early can prevent serious complications.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means that patients on anticoagulants must be educated about bleeding risks, especially those with renal impairment, to ensure timely intervention if needed.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Discontinue apixaban if she experiences any headache.**\n• Trap: Students may think that any headache warrants stopping anticoagulation therapy.\n• Why it fails here: Headaches are common and not a direct contraindication for apixaban; discontinuing it could lead to increased thromboembolic risk.\n• Remember: This option misunderstands the importance of distinguishing between side effects and serious complications.\n\n**Take apixaban with food to enhance absorption.**\n• Trap: There is a belief that all medications must be taken with food to improve their effectiveness.\n• Why it fails here: Apixaban can be taken with or without food, as its absorption is not significantly affected by food intake.\n• Remember: This option reflects a misunderstanding of the pharmacokinetics of apixaban.\n\n**Avoid all over-the-counter medications without consulting a pharmacist.**\n• Trap: Students might think that all OTC medications are unsafe with anticoagulants.\n• Why it fails here: While caution is 