management.\n• Why it fails here: Niacin is associated with significant side effects and has shown less favorable outcomes compared to other agents like ezetimibe.\n• Remember: This reinforces the importance of choosing therapies with proven efficacy and safety profiles.\n## Key takeaway\n**When managing elevated LDL cholesterol, prioritize agents like ezetimibe that effectively target cholesterol absorption without duplicating therapy.**\n\n**Memory hook:** Think 'Ezetimibe for LDL' — it’s the go-to for additional cholesterol control!","kind":"lead_in"},{"id":"cmr4jj3uy004c1ygkh0ohd6n2","old_md5":"45de72061cf08d4fc125e26f4239e275","expl":"## Why this answer is correct\nIncorporating regular aerobic exercise is essential for lowering blood pressure.\n• **Aerobic exercise** can significantly reduce blood pressure and improve heart health.\n• Regular physical activity helps with **weight management**, which is crucial for hypertension control.\n• Exercise also enhances **vascular function** and reduces stress, both of which can positively impact blood pressure.\n**In practice:** In practice, this means encouraging patients to engage in at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic exercise weekly, which can lead to better blood pressure management and overall cardiovascular health.\n## Why the other options are wrong\n\n\n\n**Increase sodium intake to improve hydration**\n• Trap: Students may think that increasing sodium can help with hydration and blood pressure.\n• Why it fails here: In fact, increasing sodium intake can lead to higher blood pressure, which is counterproductive for a patient with hypertension.\n• Remember: This option misunderstands the relationship between sodium intake and blood pressure control.\n\n**Limit fluid intake to reduce blood pressure**\n• Trap: Some may believe that reducing fluid intake is a common strategy for managing hypertension.\n• Why it fails here: Limiting fluid intake is not a standard recommendation for hypertension unle